1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,579
This is A T O Investigator, a Streamlit application for investigating account takeover.

2
00:00:05,579 --> 00:00:12,874
The project helps a fraud analyst connect login behavior, credential changes, and transfers
into one account story.

3
00:00:12,874 --> 00:00:17,595
It was built with Codex using Python, Streamlit, pandas, and Plotly.

4
00:00:17,595 --> 00:00:21,886
This edited walkthrough uses real application captures and synthetic narration.

5
00:00:21,886 --> 00:00:24,890
All customer activity shown here is fictional.

6
00:00:26,190 --> 00:00:32,023
The included dataset contains eighty accounts and one thousand five hundred seventy eight
events.

7
00:00:32,023 --> 00:00:34,523
Default rules flag twenty two accounts.

8
00:00:34,523 --> 00:00:39,107
The overview shows activity trends, detection signals, and flagged transfer exposure.

9
00:00:39,107 --> 00:00:44,107
Shared filters select an account cohort while preserving full history for detection.

10
00:00:44,107 --> 00:00:52,440
The synthetic data includes legitimate changes as well as attacks, so the dashboard
demonstrates both false positives and missed takeovers.

11
00:00:53,740 --> 00:00:57,562
The account investigation view focuses on account zero zero zero one.

12
00:00:57,562 --> 00:01:04,860
Its score is eighty, with six distinct signal reasons and a flagged transfer of six
thousand three hundred twenty two dollars.

13
00:01:04,860 --> 00:01:08,335
The score is the highest event score in account history.

14
00:01:08,335 --> 00:01:13,200
It is a reason to investigate, rather than a calibrated probability that fraud occurred.

15
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:22,890
The evidence connects a failed login burst and password reset to a successful login on a
new device in a new country.

16
00:01:22,890 --> 00:01:26,704
A beneficiary is then added, followed by a large transfer.

17
00:01:26,704 --> 00:01:31,662
The signals only timeline highlights the successful login and transfer that triggered
rules.

18
00:01:31,662 --> 00:01:35,476
All activity is also available for inspecting the preceding events.

19
00:01:35,476 --> 00:01:39,290
Detection uses only earlier observations; evaluation labels never affect scoring.

20
00:01:40,590 --> 00:01:44,842
The analyst can record evidence and next steps in case notes.

21
00:01:44,842 --> 00:01:51,412
In this demonstration, a note describing the failed logins, credential recovery, device
change, and transfer was saved.

22
00:01:51,412 --> 00:01:54,890
Review outcomes and note exports support the investigation workflow.

23
00:01:54,890 --> 00:01:57,596
Notes remain in the current Streamlit session.

24
00:01:57,596 --> 00:02:03,780
Export them before ending the session because this prototype does not have a durable case
database.

25
00:02:05,080 --> 00:02:08,588
Account zero zero one three demonstrates a legitimate lookalike.

26
00:02:08,588 --> 00:02:15,995
Travel triggers new device and new country signals, but the optional synthetic ground truth
identifies the account as legitimate.

27
00:02:15,995 --> 00:02:19,503
This is why analysts need context and alternative explanations.

28
00:02:19,503 --> 00:02:25,350
Labels are revealed only for demonstration and evaluation; they are not used by the rules.

29
00:02:26,650 --> 00:02:31,859
The detection playground exposes amount thresholds, time windows, and the account alert
threshold.

30
00:02:31,859 --> 00:02:38,271
With the default threshold of forty, the app catches eight takeovers and flags fourteen
legitimate accounts.

31
00:02:38,271 --> 00:02:40,675
Four low signal takeovers are missed.

32
00:02:40,675 --> 00:02:49,090
The confusion matrix makes those outcomes explicit, and the comparison table uses the same
account cohort for default and current rules.

33
00:02:50,390 --> 00:02:56,308
After changing the threshold to seventy and applying the rules, alerts drop from twenty two
to eight.

34
00:02:56,308 --> 00:03:01,879
Precision rises from thirty six point four percent to one hundred percent in this
engineered sample.

35
00:03:01,879 --> 00:03:07,797
Recall remains sixty six point seven percent because the same four low signal takeovers are
still missed.

36
00:03:07,797 --> 00:03:11,975
Changing a threshold cannot create evidence that is absent from the data.

37
00:03:11,975 --> 00:03:14,760
These results are not a production performance benchmark.

38
00:03:16,060 --> 00:03:20,584
The data view explains the schema and provides a dataset download.

39
00:03:20,584 --> 00:03:28,397
An uploaded C S V is validated for required columns, unique identifiers, time zones, event
types, and nonnegative amounts.

40
00:03:28,397 --> 00:03:29,631
Labels are optional.

41
00:03:29,631 --> 00:03:32,509
Streamlit processes uploads on its Python server.

42
00:03:32,509 --> 00:03:39,500
Running locally keeps that processing on your computer; a hosted app processes data on the
hosting server.

43
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:48,194
The build journal records the actual project prompts, the twelve step implementation,
source artifacts, and observed iterations.

44
00:03:48,194 --> 00:03:54,717
The user selected account takeover, then requested the Streamlit conversion and more
complete process documentation.

45
00:03:54,717 --> 00:04:02,111
The journal screenshots render those real messages and artifacts; they are not original
Codex chat interface screenshots.

46
00:04:02,111 --> 00:04:05,590
The revised report includes the full build breakdown.

47
00:04:06,890 --> 00:04:12,201
Nine Python engine tests and five Streamlit app testing workflows pass.

48
00:04:12,201 --> 00:04:20,408
They check detection behavior, label independence, time ordering, validation, filters,
investigations, notes, rule comparisons, and the journal.

49
00:04:20,408 --> 00:04:28,132
The public GitHub repository contains the working app, synthetic data, tests, screenshots,
and the revised report.

50
00:04:28,132 --> 00:04:32,960
This completes the generated walkthrough of A T O Investigator.
